#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

show_welcome() {
    cat <<'WELCOME'
SpectrumOS rescue system
Opening this live terminal does not change any disks.

Check disks and mounts:    lsblk -f    and    findmnt
Connect to the network:    nmtui
Open the recovery menu:    spectrum-recovery
  The menu offers chroot access, snapshots, Limine repair and disk checks.
  Check the root disk, EFI partition and restore point before repairing.

Installation stopped?     findmnt -R /mnt/spectrumos
Starting the erase-disk installer again repartitions the destination.
Full recovery guide:      spectrum-rescue-shell --guide
Close this terminal:      exit

WELCOME
}

show_guide() {
    cat <<'GUIDE'
SpectrumOS rescue system

Use this guide from the SpectrumOS live environment. No disks are changed on open.

Start with these checks:
  lsblk -f                         Show disks, filesystems and labels
  findmnt                          Show mounted filesystems
  nmcli general status             Check the live network connection
  nmtui                            Connect to Wi-Fi or Ethernet

For guided recovery, run:
  spectrum-recovery

The recovery menu can open an installed system with arch-chroot, list and restore
SpectrumOS snapshots, repair Limine, and show disk/SMART information. Check the
selected root disk, EFI partition and restore point before choosing a repair.
Type exit to leave a chroot or root shell and return to the recovery menu.

Inside the installed system's chroot, common repair commands include:
  pacman -Q spectrum-desktop        Check the installed desktop package
  pacman -Syu                       Update packages (requires internet)
  mkinitcpio -P                     Regenerate installed kernel images
  passwd YOUR_USERNAME             Reset the password for your account
These commands apply to the installed system only after entering its chroot.

If an installation stopped:
  findmnt -R /mnt/spectrumos        Check whether its target is still mounted
Keep the installer error output. A stopped installation is not necessarily
bootable. Starting the erase-disk installer again repartitions the destination.

Show this guide again: spectrum-rescue-shell --guide
Close this live terminal: exit

GUIDE
}

case ${1:-} in
    '')
        show_welcome
        exec bash -i
        ;;
    --guide|-h|--help) show_guide ;;
    *) printf 'Usage: %s [--guide]\n' "$0" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac
